# nodus/tuvm/tests/target/liveness-heartbeat.env — rete#231 D1, design §7 step 4
#
# THE ROW FOR THE NODE THE DETECTOR COULD NOT SEE. Every other probe on this bench arms a
# repeating timer of its own, so #215's observer always had a deadline: timer_target_snapshot
# reports the live repeaters and the shortest one bounds how long silence may last. A node
# whose APP declares no repeating timer gave it nothing — and `pulse_pump` is
# K_FOREVER-blocking on Zephyr, which is BOTH healthy-idle and wedged — so a wedge in that
# shape produced no evidence at all. A purely reactive node (GPIO, inbound radio) is the
# ordinary case for a sensor, not a hypothetical.
#
# TimerlessNodeC6 declares NO timer. The kernel registers the base tick
# (PortaBridge>>startTicking:on:, from the cadence the node declared at obs/liveness), and
# that registration is the deadline. Boot 2's TAP is the proof.
#
# THE HEARTBEAT RIDES THE REACTOR, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF D1. A heartbeat a wedged node can
# still emit is `port=up` with better manners — the exact lie #152 is about — so what this
# row must witness is not only that the detector fires, but that NOTHING SPEAKS between the
# wedge and the reboot. A check-in appearing in that window would prove the heartbeat
# outlived the wedge, i.e. that a C thread was chosen after all, and the argument for
# nsl-on-the-reactor would be wrong. Read the console between `entering the wedge` and the
# reboot banner: it must be empty of `timerless tick`.
#
# WHAT THIS ROW CANNOT SEE, said out loud:
#   - COALESCING. Design D3's claim — two obligations at different multiples of one base
#     tick cost fewer radio wakes than the sum of independent periods — needs a second
#     obligation and a wake counter, and this node has one obligation and no counter. That
#     is a separate row and it is NOT gated here; do not read this green as covering it.
#   - Whether the cadence half of goal/liveness is honoured. The kernel owns the period now,
#     but nothing re-arms the timer when an adopted cadence moves, so a requested cadence
#     still shows only as a goal/obs divergence. Also a separate row.
#   - A powered-off node versus a wedged-and-halted one. Nothing on any side can.
#
# 15 ticks x 2 s = 30 s to the wedge, 16 s of declared budget, up to 1 s of observer tick, a
# cold reboot and boot 2's first tick — call it 55 s observed, on wedge-detect's arithmetic.
# 300 s is the outer bound.
#
# OBSERVE_S outlasts the verdict on purpose (#216, and #223's exact defect): a scenario's
# TAPs passing is not the verdict, and a window that reaches one before the evidence arrives
# is a false green. 180 s, as wedge-detect.
#
# NO CONF_EXTRA — the DEFAULT build, for design §9's reason: this gate must exercise the code
# that ships, not a bench-only Kconfig split.
#
# class=disruptive: it wedges and reboots the board on purpose. A disposable board only.
SCENARIO=liveness-heartbeat
TAP_NAME=timerless
NODE=TimerlessNodeC6.nsl
BOARD_ID=esp32c6
CLASS=disruptive
TIMEOUT_S=300

OBSERVE_S=180
OBSERVE_PROGRESS='^.*timerless tick [0-9]+'
